From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 17:21:45 +0200
Subject: media: sun6i-isp: Default the bayer offsets to zero

The previous commit set these to 16 on the reading that the pipeline
loses a level by construction, the luma coefficients summing to 16383 of
16384. Measuring the coefficients with the offsets zeroed shows they sum
to exactly 16384, so the matrix is unity and the lost level is not built
into it.

What loses the level is a defect. The hardware comes out one low
whenever the matrix accumulator lands exactly on a multiple of 2^14,
unless it also lands on a multiple of 2^20. A grey field hits that on
every level, because a unity row puts the accumulator exactly on the
boundary every time, so a grey ramp reads v - 1 at 189 of 192 levels and
v at 0, 64 and 128. A single primary never hits it, which is why the
per-channel sweeps were exact all along.

An offset of 16 does hide it, and the images are better for it. It is
still the wrong place to do that: the defect belongs to the hardware,
and compensating it in the default parameters means every measurement
taken through this driver has to know that the default is not neutral --
which cost several wrong conclusions about the hardware while this was
being characterised. Userspace can set 16 if it wants the brighter
result.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
---
 drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_params.c | 33 ++++++----
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_params.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_params.c
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_params.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_params.c
@@ -24,20 +24,29 @@ static const struct sun6i_isp_params_config sun6i_isp_params_config_default = {
 
 	.bayer = {
 		/*
-		 * The pipeline loses a level by construction: the luma
-		 * coefficients sum to 16383 of 16384, so a uniform field
-		 * comes out one below what went in. These offsets are what
-		 * cancels that, and they reach the matrix divided by 16, so
-		 * 16 cancels it exactly and 32 overshoots by the same level
-		 * in the other direction.
+		 * Zero is the honest value: the luma coefficients sum to
+		 * exactly 16384, so with no offset the matrix is unity and a
+		 * uniform field should come out as it went in.
 		 *
-		 * Measured over a 192 level sweep: at 0 the output is v - 1,
-		 * at 16 it is v, and at 32 it is v + 1.
+		 * It does not, quite. The hardware drops a level whenever the
+		 * matrix accumulator lands exactly on a multiple of 2^14 --
+		 * which is every level of a grey field, since the row sums to
+		 * unity -- unless it also lands on a multiple of 2^20. So a
+		 * grey ramp reads v - 1 at 189 of 192 levels and v at 0, 64
+		 * and 128.
+		 *
+		 * An offset of 16 hides that, by shifting everything up a
+		 * level before the matrix, and this was set to 16 for a while
+		 * on that basis. It is left at 0 because the defect is the
+		 * hardware's and not the driver's, and hiding it here means
+		 * every measurement taken through this driver has to know
+		 * that the default is not neutral. Userspace can set 16 if it
+		 * wants the brighter result.
 		 */
-		.offset_r	= 16,
-		.offset_gr	= 16,
-		.offset_gb	= 16,
-		.offset_b	= 16,
+		.offset_r	= 0,
+		.offset_gr	= 0,
+		.offset_gb	= 0,
+		.offset_b	= 0,
 
 		.gain_r		= 256,
 		.gain_gr	= 256,
-- 
